@page "/"

<PageTitle>SurveyInstrument</PageTitle>

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC Survey Instrument</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This microservice manages Survey Instrument records: the directional-survey tool definitions, their embedded
    error-source snapshots, identity assignments, feature assignments, and portable versioned backups, together with
    a standalone Error Source template library. It persists to SQLite, enforces optimistic concurrency on every
    mutation, and exposes the same operations through a REST API, an MCP server, and a Blazor web application, so a
    survey instrument created or edited in one interface is immediately visible through the others.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">What is a Survey Instrument</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A <b>Survey Instrument</b> is the record for one directional-survey tool definition: a name, a
                description, server-owned creation and modification timestamps, the local field parameters it
                assumes (magnetic dip, declination and total field, Earth's gravity, latitude, grid convergence,
                Earth's rotation rate, tool cant angle), a <code>ModelType</code> selecting its error-model family, an
                embedded list of error-source snapshots, and the identity and feature assignments that describe it.
                It does not itself carry any survey stations or trajectory geometry — those live in the Trajectory
                and WellBore microservices, which reference a survey instrument by <code>SurveyInstrumentID</code> to
                obtain its error model.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Survey Instrument UUIDs are caller-generated so that consuming systems can mint and reference an
                identifier before the record exists on the server.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Four model families, magnetic and gyro</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                <code>ModelType</code> is an enforcing four-branch discriminator: <b>MWD_WolffDeWardt</b> and
                <b>Gyro_WolffDeWardt</b> implement the classical Wolff and de Wardt (1981) systematic-error model,
                with a set of optional parameters — relative depth error, misalignment, true inclination, reference
                error, drill-string magnetism, gyro-compass error — each switched on or off by its own
                <code>Use...</code> flag. <b>MWD_ISCWSA</b> and <b>Gyro_ISCWSA</b> implement the industry-standard
                ISCWSA weighting-function formulation, with gyro-specific parameters such as running speed, switching
                interval, minimum initialization distance, and noise-reduction factor.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                A survey instrument can only carry the fields that belong to its own family: create and update
                requests reject incompatible family fields as a tagged union, and the service repeats that semantic
                check before persistence rather than trusting client-side validation alone.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Error sources: the building blocks of uncertainty</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                An <b>Error Source</b> is one term of a position-uncertainty model: an <code>ErrorCode</code>, a
                magnitude expressed in the SI unit named by <code>MagnitudeQuantity</code>, systematic/random/global
                classification flags, an optional inclination interval, and the weighting functions — over
                measured depth, inclination, azimuth, and the vertical-hole special case — that turn that magnitude
                into a contribution to the position-uncertainty ellipse at a survey station.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                The standalone Error Source store is a <i>template library</i>, not a live link: each survey
                instrument embeds its own frozen <code>ErrorSourceList</code> snapshot, copied from templates at
                creation time, and later template edits never silently propagate into instruments that already copied
                them. The <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/SurveyInstrument">Survey Instrument</MudLink> page
                manages both the templates and each instrument's embedded snapshots.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Identities and features</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Every survey instrument can carry any number of user-defined <b>identities</b> — such as an official
                name, manufacturer, model, or tool name — each with an instrument-specific value, and any number of
                <b>feature</b> assignments drawn from user-defined categories and options, optionally exclusive
                within a category and optionally time-bounded. The service seeds eight default identity definitions
                and sixteen feature categories with their options on first start.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                The identity and feature <i>catalogs</i> are managed on the
                <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/SurveyInstrumentIdentities">Identities</MudLink> and
                <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/SurveyInstrumentFeatures">Features</MudLink> pages, while a
                specific instrument's assignments are added, replaced, or removed from the
                <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/SurveyInstrument">Survey Instrument</MudLink> page — a
                definition or option cannot be deleted while a survey instrument still references it.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Calculating wellbore position uncertainty in survey runs and trajectories</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        A Survey Instrument record does not compute anything by itself; it is the physics that other microservices
        apply. A <b>survey run</b>, owned by the Trajectory microservice, references exactly one survey instrument
        through its <code>SurveyInstrumentID</code>. When Trajectory evaluates position uncertainty along a
        wellbore's survey stations, it loads that instrument, reads its <code>ModelType</code>, and applies the
        matching Wolff-de-Wardt or ISCWSA weighting functions — driven by each station's measured depth,
        inclination, azimuth, and the instrument's local field parameters — to build the covariance and the
        position-uncertainty ellipse reported at that station.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Because the error-source snapshots embedded in an instrument are frozen at the time they were assigned, a
        trajectory's uncertainty calculation stays reproducible even if the underlying Error Source templates are
        later edited or removed — the same survey instrument definition always yields the same uncertainty model, and
        the <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/SurveyInstrument">Survey Instrument</MudLink> page and MCP drift
        check let an operator see exactly when a stored instrument has fallen out of sync with the current templates.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Backup and restore</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Backups are logical, portable JSON, not a raw database copy. From the
        <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/SurveyInstrumentBackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink> page,
        export <b>all</b> survey instruments or an ordered <b>selection</b> into one versioned document that carries
        the complete instruments — including their frozen embedded error-source snapshots — together with only the
        identity and feature catalog entries they actually reference and, for provenance, any standalone error-source
        templates those snapshots were copied from. Uploaded files up to 256 MiB are previewed and validated before
        an explicitly confirmed restore.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Restoring the same document back offers a <b>conflict policy</b> — fail if any survey instrument UUID already
        exists, or replace matching instruments while creating the rest — while identity and feature dependencies are
        matched by exact UUID. Validation, catalog-dependency checks, and every instrument write happen inside one
        SQLite transaction, so a restore of many instruments either commits completely or leaves the database
        untouched; there is no partial-success mode. Included standalone templates preserve provenance but never
        become live links back into the restored instruments.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Data integrity and monitoring</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Survey instrument updates, patches, and deletes use <b>optimistic concurrency</b>: every mutation must echo
        back the <code>LastModificationDate</code> (as <code>expectedModifiedUtc</code>) from the caller's latest
        read, and a stale request is rejected rather than silently overwriting a concurrent change. Because a
        standalone Error Source template carries no modification timestamp, its concurrency instead relies on a
        lowercase SHA-256 <code>versionToken</code> computed from the stored representation; MCP updates and deletes
        must supply the exact current token.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        A guarded <b>snapshot mutation</b> operation adds, replaces, or removes one embedded error-source snapshot on
        an instrument without replacing the whole array, still enforcing model-family rules — removing the last
        snapshot from an ISCWSA instrument is rejected. A <b>drift check</b> compares an instrument's frozen snapshots
        against same-UUID templates and reports each as <code>in_sync</code>, <code>drifted</code>, or
        <code>catalog_missing</code>, without modifying either side.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        <b>Catalog-reference validation and audit</b> confirm that an instrument's identity, feature-category, and
        feature-option assignment UUIDs still resolve in the local catalogs — useful after a legacy import or an
        externally corrupted database, since normal creates, updates, and restores already enforce these
        relationships. The audit is deterministic, UUID-ordered, and capped at 100 records per call. The
        <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/StatisticsSurveyInstrument">Usage Statistics</MudLink> page reports
        per-endpoint request counts for this service replica.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Using this web application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Open <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/SurveyInstrument">Survey Instrument</MudLink> to browse, search,
        create, edit, and remove survey instruments; the editor adapts to the Wolff and de Wardt or ISCWSA family
        chosen, with grouped magnetic and gyro error-term sections, and lets an instrument's identity values and
        feature assignments — including validity dates where allowed — be edited alongside its error-source
        snapshots.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Manage the shared catalogs on <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/SurveyInstrumentIdentities">Identities</MudLink>
        and <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/SurveyInstrumentFeatures">Features</MudLink>, move data between
        installations on <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/SurveyInstrumentBackupRestore">Backup and
        Restore</MudLink>, and review request volumes on
        <MudLink Href="/SurveyInstrument/webapp/StatisticsSurveyInstrument">Usage Statistics</MudLink>. All numeric
        fields follow the unit system selected for the page through the OSDC unit-reference system, while the
        service itself always exchanges SI values — radians for angles, metres for distance, m/s² for gravity, and
        tesla for magnetic flux density.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same survey instrument data is reachable through several interfaces built on the same underlying store,
        so it can be managed interactively, scripted, embedded in other Blazor applications, or wired into AI agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for browsing, editing, backing up, and
                    restoring survey instruments and error-source templates, and reviewing usage statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/SurveyInstrument/api</code> for survey instrument CRUD, metadata and
                    light-list retrieval, batch export/restore, error-source CRUD, and identity and feature catalog
                    management, documented and explorable through the Swagger UI at
                    <code>/SurveyInstrument/api/swagger</code>. Suited to scripts, backend integration, and any HTTP
                    client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at
                    <code>/SurveyInstrument/api/mcp</code> (and WebSocket at
                    <code>/SurveyInstrument/api/mcp/ws</code>), publishing 32 tools — all 27 non-statistics REST
                    operations plus five MCP-only operations (patch, guarded error-source snapshot mutation, drift
                    check, single-record catalog-reference validation, and bounded catalog-reference audit) and
                    connectivity <code>ping</code> — with strict, closed input/output JSON Schemas, so AI agents and
                    MCP-aware tooling can query, create, update, diagnose, and back up survey instruments directly.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the Survey Instrument, catalog, backup/restore, and statistics pages
                    are published as the <code>OSDC.Drilling.SurveyInstrument.WebPages</code> NuGet package, so other
                    Blazor applications — such as the Trajectory and WellBore web apps — can embed the same
                    unit-aware controls instead of re-implementing this UI.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
